Here are some of the most popular movie storytelling styles and narrative structures, including the one you mentioned:


---

🔁 1. Nonlinear Narrative (Out-of-Order Storytelling)

The story jumps around in time instead of going straight from beginning to end.

Often starts near the ending, then flashes back.

Example: Pulp Fiction, Memento



---

🔄 2. In Medias Res (“In the Middle of Things”)

The movie starts in the middle of the action (sometimes near the climax), then goes back to explain how things got there.

This is exactly the style you described.

Example: Fight Club, The Hangover



---

🔁 3. Flashback-Driven Narrative

The main story is told through flashbacks from a character’s perspective.

Often framed as a memory or retelling.

Example: Titanic, Forrest Gump



---

🔂 4. Reverse Chronological Order

The story moves backward in time, scene by scene.

You see consequences before causes.

Example: Memento



---

🔄 5. Circular Narrative (Full Circle)

The ending connects directly back to the beginning.

Sometimes the exact same scene repeats with new meaning.

Example: The Lion King



---

🧩 6. Puzzle / Fragmented Narrative

The story is intentionally confusing at first; pieces come together over time.

Viewers must actively interpret what’s happening.

Example: Inception, Donnie Darko



---

📖 7. Frame Story (Story Within a Story)

A main narrative wraps around a secondary story being told.

Example: The Princess Bride



---

🎭 8. Multiple Perspectives (Rashomon Style)

The same events are shown from different characters’ viewpoints.

Truth may be unclear or subjective.

Example: Rashomon



---

⏳ 9. Real-Time Narrative

The story unfolds in real-time with little or no time skips.

Example: 1917



---

🧠 10. Unreliable Narrator

The storyteller may be lying, mistaken, or mentally unstable.

Forces viewers to question everything.

Example: Shutter Island, Fight Club



---

